<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Greville, 2nd Baron Brooke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1607">1607</a>–<a href="/years/1643">1643</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Greville, 2nd Baron Brooke, was an English Puritan nobleman and Parliamentarian. With Viscount Saye and Sele and others he obtained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-patent">Warwick Patent</a> in 1632 for land on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, where the Saybrook Colony, named after the two lords, was founded in 1635. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> he commanded Parliamentary forces and was shot dead while besieging the cathedral close at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 1643.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-greville-2nd-baron-brooke.jpg" alt="Robert Greville, 2nd Baron Brooke" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.T. Mote · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Brooke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Greville was born in May 1607 near Helpringham, the only surviving son of Fulke Greville, an obscure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> squire, and Mary Copley. In 1611 he was adopted by his childless distant cousin Fulke Greville, 1st Baron Brooke, whose title and Warwick Castle he inherited in 1628. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from 1624 to 1627; his time in the Calvinist centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> seems to have fixed his Puritan views. In 1630 he married Catharine, daughter of Francis Russell, 4th Earl of Bedford.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected MP for Warwick in 1628, he moved to the House of Lords when he inherited his title. During the Personal Rule he joined a national network of Puritan activists and was a founder member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-island-company">Providence Island Company</a>, offering to fund the whole project in return for control, an offer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-pym">John Pym</a> rejected. He and Lord Saye dropped their plans to emigrate when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> of Massachusetts rejected their proposed constitution, insisting that only full church members should vote.</p><p>In 1639 Greville and Saye were briefly imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> for refusing to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a>. Parliament later appointed him to negotiate with the Scots, which led to the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a>, and he organised support in the Lords for the impeachment of Archbishop William Laud and the execution of Strafford. In The Nature of Truth, published in 1640, he argued that truth required belief in God but could take many forms, and that no one should be compelled to worship against conscience or reason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Made Lord Lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a> in April 1642, Greville raised funds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> that helped Parliament survive the first months of the war. On 23 August he repulsed the Earl of Northampton's attempt on Warwick Castle, and in November he helped turn back the Royalist advance on London at Brentford. After taking Stratford-upon-Avon with Sir John Gell in February 1643 he moved on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, where a sniper killed him. He was buried in the Collegiate Church of St Mary, Warwick.</p><p>Contemporaries saw his death as a serious blow: many had thought him a better commander than the Earl of Essex. His rejection of clerical power in civil affairs foreshadowed the split between Presbyterians and Independents such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, and his regiment included John Lilburne and John Okey. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a>, he influenced the later Cambridge Platonists.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Greville, 2nd Baron Brooke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1632 a group of Puritan aristocrats, led by Viscount Saye and Sele and <b>Lord Brooke</b>, obtained the Warwick Patent to land in southern New England.</blockquote><a href="/state/saybrook-colony">Saybrook Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Helpringham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.96,-0.31&amp;year=1630" aria-label="Helpringham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4543 340 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Greville, 2nd Baron Brooke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.96,-0.31&amp;year=1630">What was here in 1630? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
